-- EXPECT-ERROR: argument `k` of `run` does not satisfy precondition `k != 0`
-- Refinement types, ASSUME-WITHOUT-CHECK (2026-07-29). This is the bug that
-- the decl-walk widening (reject/t120) CREATED and this witness closes.
--
-- Once both passes walked `impl` method bodies, an impl method's parameter
-- refinement became a FACT the checker assumed: `k != 0` is what makes `m / k`
-- dischargeable under `cap no_panic`. But the table of known contracts still
-- recorded only `fn` declarations, so no caller was ever obliged to establish
-- it. `run(Box(4), 0)` therefore passed `--check` with exit 0 and the program
-- divided by zero at run time -- the exact failure `cap no_panic` exists to
-- prevent, arrived at through the very decl form the widening had just claimed
-- to cover. Walking a body and enforcing a signature are different guarantees,
-- and holding one while advertising the other is worse than holding neither:
-- the compiler walks the body, discharges the division against a contract
-- nothing enforces, and reports success.
--
-- `collect_all_defs` now registers impl-method signatures, so the obligation
-- lands here, at the call site. Registration is deliberately conservative --
-- see `adoptable_impl_methods`: a method name is adopted only when no `fn` in
-- the same module owns it and only one `impl` defines it, because a call
-- resolved by NAME cannot tell two impls' contracts apart, and checking
-- correct code against a predicate it never touches is the one failure this
-- subsystem must never have. When the name is NOT adoptable the refinement is
-- stripped from the body as well, so it binds nobody rather than binding only
-- the body (pinned by `walk-coverage` in test/test_refinecheck.ml, which the
-- corpus cannot express -- it needs two impls of one interface).
--
-- Accept companion: accept/t125, byte-for-byte this program with a satisfying
-- argument. Exit 1 here says the caller is obliged; exit 0 there says
-- obliging it did not start rejecting correct calls.
--
-- Note this fires with NO `cap` directive anywhere: a provably violated
-- obligation is reported regardless of any capability.
mod ImplContract do

  type Box = Box(Int)

  interface Runner(a) do
    fn run : a -> Int -> Int
  end

  impl Runner(Box) do
    fn run(b, k : {Int | k != 0}) : Int do
      match b do
        Box(m) -> m / k
      end
    end
  end

  fn main() do
    println(int_to_string(run(Box(4), 0)))
  end
end
